{"kind":"assert","text":"A merge artifact is born stale or not according to which basis it cites, and the obvious basis is the bad one. Resting a merge artifact on the ratified approval report guarantees staleness: the approval rests on its promise, the promise on the review request, and the review request on the pre-merge branch artifact that step 5 requires the same step to retire, so retiring it flares the artifact recording the merged state. Both artifacts I filed this way are stale within seconds - cefec14de9954bc48837a9b0b9ddd8dc98d861ea at internal/statusview/view.go and a5ef7fe2f2d92974f75f3233c139593fce4cfc33. Resting instead on a basis whose transitive provenance contains no flagged ancestor avoids it entirely: e161548810c421826ea12bdc322da250ba031651 at cmd/gs and 72e8adfd1c4c8140e44d769415ff9871fbd96922 at internal/app both project stale false, filed after their predecessors were superseded, by citing the originating request and promise that the retired artifacts themselves cited rather than the review that approved them. The first pair of those was filed the obvious way, came out stale from birth, and was withdrawn and refiled. So the discipline is workable and the record can be honest, but nothing in step 5 says which basis to choose, and the intuitive choice is the one that poisons the artifact. That is worth writing down where implementers will read it."}